    "causation_vs_correlation": {
        "definition": "Pearson correlation (r) measures the degree of linear association between two variables. It does NOT establish that one variable directly causes or determines changes in the other. Observed correlations can be driven by lurking/confounding variables, reverse causality, or joint dependency on third factors.",
        "case_studies": [
            {
                "pair": "Max Power (bhp) <--> Selling Price (r = +0.692)",
                "observation": "Cars with higher horsepower command substantially higher selling prices.",
                "why_not_direct_causation": "Confounding Variable (Luxury Segment & Brand Prestige): Bolting a high-output engine or turbocharger into an economy budget hatchback will not automatically command a 30 Lakh Rs. price. High-horsepower vehicles in this dataset are predominantly luxury and premium offerings (e.g., BMW, Mercedes-Benz, Audi, Volvo, Jaguar) that also include expensive leather interiors, advanced multi-link suspensions, panoramic roofs, and premium brand pricing power. Power serves as a proxy for the entire luxury vehicle class, rather than being the sole direct cause."
            },
            {
                "pair": "Year <--> Kilometres Driven (r = -0.388)",
                "observation": "Newer cars have lower odometer readings, while older cars have higher readings.",
                "why_not_direct_causation": "Lurking Underlying Mechanism (Cumulative Usage Duration): The calendar year changing does not physically spin the odometer wheels backwards. Rather, the passage of elapsed calendar years provides owners with more time and opportunity to drive the vehicle for daily commutes and long trips. It is the elapsed operational usage that drives mileage accumulation, not the calendar number itself."
            },
            {
                "pair": "Seats <--> Mileage (kmpl) (r = -0.479)",
                "observation": "Vehicles with 7 or 8 seats exhibit significantly lower fuel economy than 5-seater cars.",
                "why_not_direct_causation": "Structural Vehicle Architecture: Installing two extra seats into a vehicle cabin does not mechanically degrade the thermodynamic combustion inside the engine cylinders. Instead, 7-seater vehicles (such as Toyota Innova or Mahindra Scorpio) are engineered with substantially heavier chassis, larger frontal surface area (aerodynamic drag), and higher-displacement engines. It is this structural weight and engine displacement that causes lower mileage, not the presence of the physical seat cushions."
            }
        ]
